{
  "dom_id": "question-133230",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for our polar curve.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPreviously, we've found that\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx}) & =\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta )) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
